# How this deck was made (W3C PROV-O): the activities and sources below.
<#compilation>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-14T12:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:used <https://en.wiktionary.org/> ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith <#anton-wiklund> ;
    prov:wasInformedBy <#review-0> ,
                       <#review-1> ,
                       <#review-2> ;
    rdfs:comment "Compiled by Anton Wiklund with the help of AI."@en ,
                 "Sammanställd av Anton Wiklund med hjälp av AI."@sv ,
                 "The deck was checked by machine (scripts checking the count, the ids, NFC and the uniqueness of every meaning in every language; the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators and a second SHACL validator) and by AI agents; it has had no human review."@en ,
                 "Selection: 156 cards in ten groups: greetings and their standard replies, courtesy, answers (yes, no, maybe), introductions, questions, simple needs, the numbers 0 to 10 with 20, 100 and 1000, the days of the week, time words, and the set expressions every learner meets (إِنْ شَاءَ اللّٰه, الْحَمْدُ لِلّٰه, مَا شَاءَ اللّٰه, بِسْمِ اللّٰه), whose notes say how they are used, not what to believe. The list is the authoring agent's own, in Modern Standard Arabic throughout."@en ,
                 "Writing the cards. The front is the phrase in Arabic, fully voweled, as said at a pause (a case vowel the pause drops is left off, a vowel that stays, as in شُكْرًا, is kept), in the form said to a man, the form to a woman or to several in the note where it differs. The back gives one natural equivalent in each of six languages, unique in each. The note starts with the transliteration in ALA-LC letters (ā ī ū, th kh dh sh gh, ʻ for ayn, ʼ for hamza, no initial hamza, ta marbuta at a pause -ah), the article written as it is said (as-salāmu, ṣabāḥu l-khayr), then, where it helps, the usual reply, the register or how everyday speech differs. Each phrase was confirmed against its English Wiktionary entry, or its words' entries, where Wiktionary has them."@en ,
                 "Card ids are the transliteration in ASCII letters, without diacritics, ʻ or ʼ, its words joined by hyphens (as-salamu-alaykum, shukran, kayfa-haluka). They are addresses, not shown as text."@en ,
                 "Licensing: the deck is CC BY-SA 4.0, as English Wiktionary (CC BY-SA 4.0), against which its phrases were checked, is; it is credited here."@en .

<#review-0>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-14T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Arabic and transliteration (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 156 phrases and the Arabic quoted in their notes, checked by an AI agent letter by letter and mark by mark: correct, idiomatic Modern Standard Arabic, fully voweled, at a pause, the transliteration matching the Arabic; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No errors: the Arabic, its vowel marks and pause forms, the transliterations and the Arabic quoted in the notes were all found right."@en .

<#review-1>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-14T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Meanings and notes in six languages (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 156 cards' meanings and notes in English, Swedish, Korean, German, Spanish and French, checked by an AI agent for natural everyday equivalents, uniqueness, and notes that say the same in every language; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 10 corrections: meanings that were word-for-word glosses rather than what speakers say (Korean and Swedish for مَا الْأَخْبَار, German wishes that were fragments), two literal glosses in the notes for مَعَ السَّلَامَة that read as 'certainly' in German and Spanish, notes for تَفَضَّل brought in line across the languages, and a Korean note that read 'girlfriend'."@en .

<#review-2>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-14T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Machine checks"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: The whole release: the scripts' checks, the library checker (npm run library) with the app's shapes, DCAT-AP and SKOS, and a second SHACL validator (pySHACL, npm run crosscheck)."@en ,
                 "Outcome: The release conforms: the scripts found every meaning unique in each language and every text in NFC; the library checker found the library valid with this deck in it, and pySHACL found all 228 documents it checks conform."@en .

